{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1482310435,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUxOTY4Nzc2OTI=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1175","origin":"github"},"title":"Fate of `if_dirty` in the light of #1169 and other API RFs","message":"I would appreciate your feedback on how to handle `if_dirty` in the various API commands. It was added because we aimed for \"always-clean\" datasets. A result of our recent discussions was that we aim to be able to deal with any amount of dirtyness and do \"surgical\" operations.\n\nConsequently, I propose to remove `if_dirty` form all commands. It adds a serious burden regarding the serial order of processing that needs to be applies and complicates dataset interdependencies. So unless there is a very strong argument, why we cannot live without it, I see no reason to pay the complexity price for it.\n\nQuickish feedback would be appreciated @bpoldrack @yarikoptic","files":null}]}